          <div style="background:var(--bg-surface);border:1px solid var(--neon-yellow);border-radius:6px;padding:0.8rem 1rem;margin-bottom:0.6rem;">
            <div style="font-size:0.75rem;color:var(--neon-yellow);font-weight:bold;margin-bottom:0.3rem;">STEP 2 · Transformation Stage</div>
            <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:0.5rem;background:rgba(255,255,255,0.03);padding:0.5rem;border-radius:4px;border:1px dashed var(--border-dim);">
              <span style="font-size:1.1rem;">🗃️</span>
              <span style="font-size:0.86rem;color:var(--text-primary);font-weight:500;">Activity: SQL Query</span>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Arrow -->
          <div style="display:flex;justify-content:center;color:var(--neon-yellow);margin:0.2rem 0;">
            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"><line x1="12" y1="4" x2="12" y2="20"></line><polyline points="18 14 12 20 6 14"></polyline></svg>
          </div>

          <!-- Step 3 -->
          <div style="background:var(--bg-surface);border:1px solid var(--neon-yellow);border-radius:6px;padding:0.8rem 1rem;">
            <div style="font-size:0.75rem;color:var(--neon-yellow);font-weight:bold;margin-bottom:0.3rem;">STEP 3 · Send Stage</div>
            <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:0.5rem;background:rgba(255,255,255,0.03);padding:0.5rem;border-radius:4px;border:1px dashed var(--border-dim);">
              <span style="font-size:1.1rem;">✉️</span>
              <span style="font-size:0.86rem;color:var(--text-primary);font-weight:500;">Activity: Send Email</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="callout tip" style="margin-top:1.2rem;">
        <div class="callout-label">💡 IN A NUTSHELL</div>
        An <strong>Automation</strong> is the complete workflow; a <strong>Step</strong> is a stage within it; and <strong>Activities</strong> are the discrete tasks making up each stage.
      </div>

      <!-- SECTION 04 EN -->
      <h2 id="sec-starting-sources-en">04 · HOW DOES AN AUTOMATION START?</h2>
      <p>
        Every automation requires a trigger to determine how and when it starts. According to current official Salesforce documentation, there are <strong>three official Starting Sources</strong>:
      </p>

      <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(280px,1fr));gap:1rem;margin:1.5rem 0;">
        <div style="background:var(--bg-elevated);border:1px solid var(--border-dim);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:1.2rem;">
          <div style="font-family:var(--font-arcade);font-size:0.48rem;color:var(--neon-yellow);margin-bottom:0.5rem;">⏰ 1. SCHEDULE</div>
          <p style="font-size:0.86rem;color:var(--text-secondary);margin:0 0 0.5rem 0;">
            Starts an Automation at defined recurring intervals (e.g. daily at 02:00 AM, hourly, or every Monday).
          </p>
          <span style="font-size:0.75rem;color:var(--neon-green);">Example: Nightly audience calculations.</span>
        </div>

        <div style="background:var(--bg-elevated);border:1px solid var(--border-dim);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:1.2rem;">
          <div style="font-family:var(--font-arcade);font-size:0.48rem;color:var(--neon-orange);margin-bottom:0.5rem;">📂 2. FILE DROP</div>
          <p style="font-size:0.86rem;color:var(--text-secondary);margin:0 0 0.5rem 0;">
            Starts an Automation when a file is uploaded to the Enhanced FTP according to defined criteria (such as a filename pattern or specific directory).
          </p>
          <span style="font-size:0.75rem;color:var(--neon-cyan);">Example: A partner uploads <code>customers.csv</code>.</span>
        </div>

        <div style="background:var(--bg-elevated);border:1px solid var(--border-dim);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:1.2rem;">
          <div style="font-family:var(--font-arcade);font-size:0.48rem;color:var(--neon-pink);margin-bottom:0.5rem;">⚡ 3. TRIGGER</div>
          <p style="font-size:0.86rem;color:var(--text-secondary);margin:0 0 0.5rem 0;">
            Starts an Automation when a file is uploaded to a supported external cloud location (such as Amazon S3, Microsoft Azure Blob Storage, or Google Cloud Storage) and a request is made to the Trigger API.
          </p>
          <span style="font-size:0.75rem;color:var(--neon-pink);">Example: File dropped in external bucket with Trigger API request.</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Subsection: Manual Execution -->
      <h3 style="color:var(--text-primary);font-size:1.05rem;margin-top:1.5rem;">Manual Execution</h3>
      <p>
        In addition to the three scheduled and event-driven Starting Sources, Automation Studio provides on-demand manual execution options:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Run Once</strong>: Allows you to manually start an entire Automation on demand from the Automation Studio interface, ideal for testing or ad-hoc runs.</li>
        <li><strong>Advanced Run Once</strong>: Allows you to select only specific Steps or Activities for partial execution. Salesforce documents this capability specifically for <em>Scheduled Automations</em>.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout tip">
        <div class="callout-label">💡 PRO TIP: PROGRAMMATIC EXECUTION VIA API</div>
        Automation Studio also provides REST and SOAP APIs documented on Salesforce Developers to start, pause, or manage Automations programmatically. This integration capability is distinct from the <strong>Trigger</strong> Starting Source explained above.
      </div>

      <div class="callout tip" style="margin-top:1.2rem;">
        <div class="callout-label">💡 IN A NUTSHELL</div>
        There are three official Starting Sources (Schedule, File Drop, and Trigger). Options like Run Once represent manual on-demand execution mechanisms.
      </div>

      <!-- SECTION 05 EN -->
      <h2 id="sec-activity-families-en">05 · MAIN FAMILIES OF ACTIVITIES</h2>
      <p>
        An <strong>Activity</strong> is the basic building block that performs discrete work inside an Automation. For educational purposes, in Marketing Cloud Arcade we group Activities into the following <strong>conceptual families</strong> (note that this is a pedagogical grouping rather than an official Salesforce taxonomy):
      </p>

      <div style="background:var(--bg-elevated);border:1px solid var(--border-dim);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:1.2rem;margin:1.5rem 0;">
        <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:1.2rem;">

          <div>
            <div style="color:var(--neon-green);font-weight:bold;font-size:0.95rem;margin-bottom:0.3rem;">1. Data Management and Files</div>
            <p style="font-size:0.88rem;color:var(--text-secondary);margin:0 0 0.4rem 0;">
              Activities dedicated to moving, staging, and loading tabular records.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size:0.85rem;color:var(--text-secondary);margin:0;padding-left:1.2rem;">
              <li><strong>Data Copy or Import (formerly Import File)</strong>: Imports data from external files and, in specific scenarios, copies data between Data Extensions.</li>
              <li><strong>File Transfer Activity</strong>: Allows you to move, unzip, encrypt, or decrypt files based on configuration. For example, it can manage files in a configured location (Manage File) or move files from the Safehouse to an FTP location.</li>
              <li><strong>Data Extract Activity</strong>: Extracts data from a Data Extension or generates files containing tracking data (opens, clicks, bounces) into the Safehouse.</li>
              <li><strong>Filter Activity</strong>: Applies filtering conditions to a Data Extension to create a segmented subset.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div style="border-top:1px solid var(--border-dim);padding-top:1rem;">
            <div style="color:var(--neon-yellow);font-weight:bold;font-size:0.95rem;margin-bottom:0.3rem;">2. Queries and Code</div>
            <p style="font-size:0.88rem;color:var(--text-secondary);margin:0 0 0.4rem 0;">
              Analytical data transformations and programmatic logic.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size:0.85rem;color:var(--text-secondary);margin:0;padding-left:1.2rem;">
              <li><strong>SQL Query Activity</strong>: Executes SQL queries to retrieve, combine, and transform records from Data Extensions or Data Views into a target Data Extension.</li>
              <li><strong>Script Activity</strong>: Runs Server-Side JavaScript (SSJS) on the server for custom logic or programmatic integrations.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div style="border-top:1px solid var(--border-dim);padding-top:1rem;">
            <div style="color:var(--neon-orange);font-weight:bold;font-size:0.95rem;margin-bottom:0.3rem;">3. Messaging and Sends</div>
            <p style="font-size:0.88rem;color:var(--text-secondary);margin:0 0 0.4rem 0;">
              Bulk communication dispatches.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size:0.85rem;color:var(--text-secondary);margin:0;padding-left:1.2rem;">
              <li><strong>Send Email</strong>: Initiates email sends to a target audience prepared by upstream steps.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div style="border-top:1px solid var(--border-dim);padding-top:1rem;">
            <div style="color:var(--neon-cyan);font-weight:bold;font-size:0.95rem;margin-bottom:0.3rem;">4. Flow Control and Governance</div>
            <p style="font-size:0.88rem;color:var(--text-secondary);margin:0 0 0.4rem 0;">
              Timing control and data quality verification.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size:0.85rem;color:var(--text-secondary);margin:0;padding-left:1.2rem;">
              <li><strong>Wait Activity</strong>: Pauses execution for a set duration or until a specific date and time.</li>
              <li><strong>Verification Activity</strong>: Evaluates the record count of a target Data Extension. When the count meets configured conditions, it can stop the Automation, send an email notification, or do both.<br/><span style="font-size:0.8rem;color:var(--text-muted);display:block;margin-top:0.25rem;"><em>Beginner note:</em> Verification does not directly evaluate specific field values (such as <code>Status == 'Active'</code>). If you need to validate specific data conditions, you typically prepare those records using SQL first and then verify the resulting Data Extension record count.</span></li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div style="border-top:1px solid var(--border-dim);padding-top:1rem;">
            <div style="color:var(--neon-pink);font-weight:bold;font-size:0.95rem;margin-bottom:0.3rem;">5. Relationship with Journey Builder</div>
            <p style="font-size:0.88rem;color:var(--text-secondary);margin:0 0 0.4rem 0;">
              Automation Studio can prepare data utilized subsequently by Journey Builder. Salesforce still documents <strong>Fire Event Activity</strong> for Contact Data Event scenarios, but warns that Contact Data Events will no longer be supported in an upcoming release (existing journeys continue functioning). For new implementations, consult current Journey Builder entry mechanisms like <strong>Data Extensions</strong> or <strong>Audiences</strong> depending on the scenario. The official Automation Studio Activities reference also includes <strong>Journey Entry: Audience</strong> for automations that update entry audiences.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="callout">
        <div class="callout-label">📌 ACTIVITY REUSABILITY</div>
        Many configured Activities can be reused and appear across multiple Automations. However, specific exceptions exist: for example, <strong>Verification Activity</strong> is configured within a single Automation and cannot be reused across multiple Automations.
      </div>

      <div class="callout tip" style="margin-top:1.2rem;">
        <div class="callout-label">💡 IN A NUTSHELL</div>
        Activities are the discrete tasks of an Automation. Each type solves a specific need: transferring files, querying data, sending messages, or verifying quality.
      </div>

      <!-- SECTION 06 EN -->
      <h2 id="sec-sql-role-en">06 · THE ROLE OF SQL QUERY ACTIVITY</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>SQL Query Activity</strong> is one of the most widely used Activities for retrieving, combining, filtering, and preparing data in Automation Studio.
      </p>
      <p>
        Its primary purpose is to read records from one or more Data Extensions (or system Data Views), execute an SQL query to transform or merge them, and store the result in a target Data Extension.
      </p>

      <!-- DIAGRAM B EN: SQL QUERY ACTIVITY (HTML/CSS) -->
      <div style="background:var(--bg-elevated);border:1px solid var(--border-dim);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:1.5rem;margin:1.5rem 0;">
        <div style="font-family:var(--font-arcade);font-size:0.48rem;color:var(--neon-yellow);margin-bottom:1rem;text-align:center;">
          🗃️ DATA FLOW IN SQL QUERY ACTIVITY
        </div>
        
