      <div class="callout warning">
        <div class="callout-label">⚠️ SQL IS NOT AUTOMATION STUDIO</div>
        It is essential to distinguish between the two: <strong>SQL</strong> is the query language, and <strong>SQL Query Activity</strong> is one of the many Activities that Automation Studio executes. We will master SQL queries in a dedicated upcoming article.
      </div>

      <div class="callout tip" style="margin-top:1.2rem;">
        <div class="callout-label">💡 IN A NUTSHELL</div>
        SQL Query Activity executes an SQL query to read, transform, or prepare data stored in Marketing Cloud.
      </div>

      <!-- SECTION 07 EN -->
      <h2 id="sec-transfer-vs-import-en">07 · FILE TRANSFER VS DATA COPY OR IMPORT</h2>
      <p>
        A frequent point of confusion among beginners is distinguishing between <strong>File Transfer Activity</strong> and <strong>Data Copy or Import</strong> (formerly known as <em>Import File</em>). While both are part of ingestion pipelines, their responsibilities are distinct and complementary:
      </p>

      <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(280px,1fr));gap:1rem;margin:1.5rem 0;">
        <div style="background:var(--bg-elevated);border:1px solid var(--border-dim);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:1.2rem;">
          <div style="font-family:var(--font-arcade);font-size:0.48rem;color:var(--neon-orange);margin-bottom:0.5rem;">🚚 FILE TRANSFER ACTIVITY</div>
          <div style="font-size:0.85rem;color:var(--text-secondary);margin-bottom:0.6rem;">
            <strong>"Works with the physical file"</strong>
          </div>
          <p style="font-size:0.85rem;color:var(--text-secondary);line-height:1.5;margin:0;">
            Moves, unzips, encrypts, or decrypts files depending on configured options (such as <em>Manage File</em> or <em>Move a File From Safehouse</em>). Manages the physical container or file object in storage.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div style="background:var(--bg-elevated);border:1px solid var(--border-dim);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:1.2rem;">
          <div style="font-family:var(--font-arcade);font-size:0.48rem;color:var(--neon-green);margin-bottom:0.5rem;">📦 DATA COPY OR IMPORT</div>
          <div style="font-size:0.85rem;color:var(--text-secondary);margin-bottom:0.6rem;">
            <strong>"Works with the records and data"</strong>
          </div>
          <p style="font-size:0.85rem;color:var(--text-secondary);line-height:1.5;margin:0;">
            Imports records from an external file into a Data Extension or list, or copies data across Data Extensions. A standard CSV file does not strictly need to pass through File Transfer before being imported.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="callout tip">
        <div class="callout-label">📦 THE PACKAGE ANALOGY</div>
        • <strong>File Transfer</strong> prepares or transports the package (moving, unzipping, or decrypting it).<br/>
        • <strong>Data Copy or Import</strong> takes the data inside the package and loads or copies it into target structures.<br/><br/>
        Conceptually, <em>File Transfer</em> manages the physical container, whereas <em>Data Copy or Import</em> processes the rows and records inside.
      </div>

      <div class="callout tip" style="margin-top:1.2rem;">
        <div class="callout-label">💡 IN A NUTSHELL</div>
        File Transfer prepares or transports the package; Data Copy or Import takes data from the package and loads it into target tables.
      </div>

      <!-- SECTION 08 EN -->
      <h2 id="sec-execution-order-en">08 · SEQUENTIAL STEPS & SIMULTANEOUS ACTIVITIES</h2>
      <p>
        Automation Studio allows you to organize activities sequentially or concurrently based on how they are arranged across Steps:
      </p>

      <div style="background:var(--bg-elevated);border:1px solid var(--border-dim);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:1.2rem;margin:1.5rem 0;">
        <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:1rem;">
          <div>
            <span style="color:var(--neon-yellow);font-weight:bold;font-size:0.92rem;">1. STEPS execute sequentially</span>
            <p style="font-size:0.86rem;color:var(--text-secondary);margin:0.3rem 0 0 0;">
              Step 1 executes first. Once its activities complete, the Automation continues to Step 2, and so on.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div style="border-top:1px solid var(--border-dim);padding-top:0.8rem;">
            <span style="color:var(--neon-green);font-weight:bold;font-size:0.92rem;">2. Multiple ACTIVITIES in the same Step can execute simultaneously</span>
            <p style="font-size:0.86rem;color:var(--text-secondary);margin:0.3rem 0 0 0;">
              If you place multiple activities in the same Step, Automation Studio will attempt to run them in parallel at the same time.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="callout warning">
        <div class="callout-label">⚠️ DEPENDENCY PRINCIPLE BETWEEN TASKS</div>
        Order matters when one task depends on the output of another. If <strong>Activity B</strong> requires the data generated by <strong>Activity A</strong>, <strong>they must be placed in consecutive Steps</strong>, never in the same Step.
        <br/><br/>
        <em>Correct example:</em><br/>
        • <strong>Step 1:</strong> SQL Query generates <code>Audience_Final</code>.<br/>
        • <strong>Step 2:</strong> Send Email uses <code>Audience_Final</code>.
        <br/><br/>
        Analogy: <em>"You cannot pack a product before manufacturing it"</em>.
      </div>

      <p style="font-size:0.88rem;color:var(--text-secondary);">
        <strong>Error behavior:</strong> When an Automation encounters errors in an Activity, execution can stop and subsequent Steps may not execute. Detailed behavior and resulting statuses should be consulted in <em>Automation History</em> and Automation Studio alerts.
      </p>

      <div class="callout tip" style="margin-top:1.2rem;">
        <div class="callout-label">💡 IN A NUTSHELL</div>
        If an Activity depends on another Activity's result, the Step order matters: place them in consecutive Steps.
      </div>

      <!-- SECTION 09 EN -->
      <h2 id="sec-data-extensions-en">09 · DATA EXTENSIONS: THE WORKBENCHES</h2>
      <p>
        Many Automations utilize <strong>Data Extensions (DE)</strong> as the source, destination, or staging area for data.
      </p>
      <p>
        In automation design practice, three conceptual roles for Data Extensions are common:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Staging (Intermediate area)</strong>: Receives raw data directly as imported from an external file.</li>
        <li><strong>Transformation</strong>: Auxiliary tables for intermediate calculations or temporary joins.</li>
        <li><strong>Target Audience</strong>: The clean, final dataset consumed by a message send or a Journey.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout tip">
        <div class="callout-label">🪑 THE WORKSHOP ANALOGY</div>
        Data Extensions act as <strong>the workbenches or storage shelves</strong> where different Activities read, store, and pick up information throughout the process.
      </div>

      <div class="callout tip" style="margin-top:1.2rem;">
        <div class="callout-label">💡 IN A NUTSHELL</div>
        Data Extensions are the storage structures where Activities read and persist processed data.
      </div>

      <!-- SECTION 10 EN -->
      <h2 id="sec-full-example-en">10 · COMPLETE AUTOMATION WALKTHROUGH</h2>
      <p>
        Let's look at a straightforward end-to-end example: <strong>daily VIP customer audience preparation</strong>.
      </p>

      <!-- DIAGRAM C EN: COMPLETE WALKTHROUGH (HTML/CSS) -->
      <div style="background:var(--bg-elevated);border:1px solid var(--border-dim);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:1.5rem;margin:1.5rem 0;">
        <div style="font-family:var(--font-arcade);font-size:0.48rem;color:var(--neon-green);margin-bottom:1rem;text-align:center;">
          🚀 COMPLETE WORKFLOW: DAILY VIP AUDIENCE
        </div>
        
        <div style="max-width:540px;margin:0 auto;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0.4rem;">
          <!-- Trigger -->
          <div style="background:rgba(255,230,0,0.08);border:1px solid var(--neon-yellow);border-radius:6px;padding:0.8rem;text-align:center;">
            <div style="font-size:0.75rem;color:var(--neon-yellow);font-weight:bold;">STARTING SOURCE · SCHEDULE</div>
            <div style="font-size:0.85rem;color:var(--text-primary);">Every day at 02:00 AM</div>
          </div>

          <div style="display:flex;justify-content:center;color:var(--neon-cyan);"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"><line x1="12" y1="2" x2="12" y2="18"></line><polyline points="18 12 12 18 6 12"></polyline></svg></div>

          <!-- Step 1 -->
          <div style="background:rgba(255,255,255,0.03);border:1px solid var(--border-dim);border-radius:6px;padding:0.8rem;text-align:center;">
            <div style="font-size:0.75rem;color:var(--neon-orange);font-weight:bold;">STEP 1 · DATA COPY OR IMPORT</div>
            <div style="font-size:0.85rem;color:var(--text-primary);">Import new customer records into "Staging_Customers" DE</div>
          </div>

          <div style="display:flex;justify-content:center;color:var(--neon-cyan);"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"><line x1="12" y1="2" x2="12" y2="18"></line><polyline points="18 12 12 18 6 12"></polyline></svg></div>

          <!-- Step 2 -->
          <div style="background:rgba(255,255,255,0.03);border:1px solid var(--border-dim);border-radius:6px;padding:0.8rem;text-align:center;">
            <div style="font-size:0.75rem;color:var(--neon-yellow);font-weight:bold;">STEP 2 · SQL QUERY ACTIVITY</div>
            <div style="font-size:0.85rem;color:var(--text-primary);">Execute query to identify and segment VIP customers</div>
          </div>

          <div style="display:flex;justify-content:center;color:var(--neon-cyan);"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"><line x1="12" y1="2" x2="12" y2="18"></line><polyline points="18 12 12 18 6 12"></polyline></svg></div>

          <!-- Outcome -->
          <div style="background:rgba(0,255,136,0.08);border:1.5px solid var(--neon-green);border-radius:6px;padding:0.8rem;text-align:center;">
            <div style="font-size:0.75rem;color:var(--neon-green);font-weight:bold;">FINAL OUTCOME</div>
            <div style="font-size:0.9rem;color:var(--text-primary);font-weight:bold;">DE "Audience_VIP" refreshed and ready for send</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        <strong>What happens in each stage?</strong>
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Schedule</strong>: Starts the Automation at the planned time (02:00 AM).</li>
        <li><strong>Step 1</strong>: Ingests new customer data into an intermediate staging Data Extension.</li>
        <li><strong>Step 2</strong>: The SQL query joins staging data, evaluates VIP criteria, and writes output into the final audience DE.</li>
        <li><strong>Outcome</strong>: <code>Audience_VIP</code> is refreshed and available for activation.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout tip" style="margin-top:1.2rem;">
        <div class="callout-label">💡 IN A NUTSHELL</div>
        A typical automation combines a starting source, ingestion activities, query activities, and a target Data Extension prepared for downstream action.
      </div>

      <!-- SECTION 11 EN -->
